Action: select_stable  
Action Input: {"k": 7}

Thought: The ranking is unstable and many models are highly correlated; a robust median over a pruned, non‑redundant subset should outperform the current leader.  
Action: prune_redundant  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool_full","corr_threshold":0.95,"metric":"rmse"}

Thought: The current leader is a trimmed mean on pool1; a robust alternative is a simple median over the same stable pool, which may reduce influence of any remaining outliers.  
Action: evaluate_strategy  
Action Input: {"combine":"median","pool":"pool1","rationale":"robust median on the most stable subset"}

Thought: The current leader uses trimmed mean on pool1; try a more aggressive weighted inverse‑error scheme on the same pool to see if it can beat the trimmed mean while staying robust to the unstable ranking.  
Action: weights_inverse_error  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool1","metric":"rmse","shrinkage":0.0}

Thought: Test a simple inverse-error weighted average on the stable pool1 to see if it beats the trimmed_mean leader.  
Action: evaluate_strategy  
Action Input: {"combine":"weighted","pool":"pool1","weights":"w2","rationale":"inverse-error weights on the stable subset"}

Thought: The current best is trimmed_mean pool1. Try inverse-error weights with moderate shrinkage to reduce variance from few low-error models.  
Action: weights_inverse_error  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool1","metric":"rmse","shrinkage":0.5}

Thought: Test the raw inverse‑error weights (shrinkage 0.5) on the stable pool to see if a slightly more uniform weighting improves over the trimmed mean.  
Action: evaluate_strategy  
Action Input: {"combine":"weighted","pool":"pool1","weights":"w3","rationale":"raw inverse-error weights on the stable subset"}

Thought: The current best is trimmed_mean on pool1. Try a weighted combination on the pruned pool4, which has fewer correlated models and may improve performance.  
Action: weights_inverse_error  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool4","metric":"rmse","shrinkage":0.0}

Thought: The error correlation is very high (0.91) and many models are redundant; pruning the redundant groups should reduce noise and improve a weighted combination.  
Action: prune_redundant  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool_full","corr_threshold":0.95,"metric":"rmse"}

Thought: The current leader uses a trimmed mean on a small stable pool. To explore a different robust approach, try Caruana forward selection weights on the pruned pool4, which may capture complementary strengths while remaining robust.  
Action: weights_forward  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool4","max_steps":20,"metric":"rmse"}

Thought: Try inverse‑error weights with moderate shrinkage on the stable pool to reduce variance while still favoring low‑error models.  
Action: evaluate_strategy  
Action Input: {"combine":"weighted","pool":"pool1","weights":"w2","rationale":"inverse-error with shrinkage on stable subset"}

Thought: Try inverse‑error weighting on the more stable pool2 with moderate shrinkage to reduce variance from only 3 windows.  
Action: weights_inverse_error  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool2","metric":"rmse","shrinkage":0.5,"per_horizon":false}

Thought: Test inverse‑error weights on the larger, more diverse pool2 to see if a broader weighted mean beats the trimmed‑mean leader.

Action: evaluate_strategy

Action Input: {"combine":"weighted","pool":"pool2","weights":"w6","rationale":"inverse‑error weights on pool2"}

Thought: Try a cross‑series meta‑model weight on the reduced pool4 to see if dataset‑level knowledge improves over simple inverse‑error weights.  
Action: weights_meta_model  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool4","scope":"dataset","metric":"smape"}

Thought: The best validated strategy is the trimmed_mean on pool1 (score 0.6375).  
Action: accept  
Action Input: {}

